#!ipxe
{# ${netX/mac}, ${net0/mac:hexraw} etc. below are iPXE runtime variables, NOT
   Jinja2 - they're resolved by iPXE on the booting host at boot time, not by
   Ansible at template time. Wrapped in {% raw %} so Jinja leaves them alone;
   only pxe_server_vm_ip is templated by Ansible. Letting Jinja touch these
   is the single most likely bug in this role - it would silently render an
   empty or garbage lookup key and break every host's PXE boot.

   EVERY NIC's MAC is tried, not just net0's. install-server maps only the
   BareMetalHost's boot MAC (the first link-up NIC server-scan lists), and the
   firmware loads the full ipxe.efi, which drives every NIC: net0 is merely
   the first one iPXE enumerated, not necessarily the one that booted or the
   one that was mapped. netX (the device iPXE last opened - the one that got
   DHCP and fetched this script) goes first, then net0..net3. Each lookup that
   finds no file is a 404 and falls through via `||`; an absent device expands
   to an empty key (`/pxe-map/.ipxe`), which is a 404 as well. Every MAC
   tried is this host's own, so whichever one is mapped is this host's
   mapping. -#}
{% raw -%}
echo Booting NIC MAC: ${netX/mac} (also trying net0-net3)
chain http://{% endraw %}{{ pxe_server_vm_ip }}{% raw %}/pxe-map/${netX/mac:hexraw}.ipxe ||
chain http://{% endraw %}{{ pxe_server_vm_ip }}{% raw %}/pxe-map/${net0/mac:hexraw}.ipxe ||
chain http://{% endraw %}{{ pxe_server_vm_ip }}{% raw %}/pxe-map/${net1/mac:hexraw}.ipxe ||
chain http://{% endraw %}{{ pxe_server_vm_ip }}{% raw %}/pxe-map/${net2/mac:hexraw}.ipxe ||
chain http://{% endraw %}{{ pxe_server_vm_ip }}{% raw %}/pxe-map/${net3/mac:hexraw}.ipxe ||
goto failed
:failed
echo No PXE mapping found for any NIC: ${net0/mac:hexraw} ${net1/mac:hexraw} ${net2/mac:hexraw} ${net3/mac:hexraw}
echo Rebooting in 30 seconds...
sleep 30
reboot
{% endraw %}
